Because of the unique acceptance of the STAR TPC and BEMC, any data analysis dependent on an accurate measure of relative particle multiplicities, in different sectors of the \(\eta-\phi\) space (such as a correlation analysis), will be biased towards particles produced near the center of the detector volume (where the acceptance approaches unity). Such an analysis will therefore need to be corrected to account for this varying acceptance. This correction is called pair acceptance correction, because it uses a distribution of uncorrelated pairs of particles to correct for the acceptance shape.

Mixed pairs are generated by randomly sampling separate histograms of trigger and associated particle \(\eta-\phi\) distributions, filled in 22 vertex bins of 5 cm width (from -55 to 55 cm) and in the 5 \(p_{T}\) bins described in table V.1. Both the trigger and associated distribution histograms are filled simultaneously with the real pairs, so they are subject to the same trigger and associated cuts outlined in Sec. V.2.1.

