### \(\chi^{2}\) Analysis of Ridge Yields

The conclusion from the initial ridge results[10] is that the ridge yield is constant (and non-zero) for all trigger \(p_{T}\) values. Certainly at low \(p_{T}\) (in the charged-particle triggered correlations) this appears to be a fair assumption, however in light of the new results presented in this work, it is not clear that that conclusion remains valid for the \(\pi^{0}\)-triggered correlations at higher \(p_{T}\). But, because of the relatively large statistical and systematic uncertainties in these new results; it is difficult to make a definitive statement about trends in the ridge yields at higher \(p_{T}\). A quantitative measure of how the data trends is therefore needed. This quantitative measure can be defined as a variation of a \(\chi^{2}\) sum, in which a weighted (by the variance on this error) sum-of-the-squares of the"error" (the difference between the observed yield value and the expected yield value) is calculated.

The first step in this analysis is to fit, with a constant value, the ridge yields in the charged-particle triggered correlation. This best estimate of a constant yield will be used as the expected value in the \(\chi^{2}\) calculation at higher \(p_{T}\). To allow for independent assessment of the different yield determination methods, and of the impact on the increased statistics of Run 10; a separate expected yield value is extracted for each method in each running year. These values and the percent error of the fit are tabulated in Table VI.7.

The expected values from Tab. VI.7 are used as the value \(E_{i}\) in the \(\chi^{2}\) "goodness-of-fit" equation,

\[\chi^{2}_{f}=\sum_{i}{(O_{i}-E_{i})^{2}\over\sigma_{i}^{2}},\]\[\chi_{0}^{2}=\sum_{i}{O_{i}^{2}\over\sigma_{i}^{2}},\]

where \(O_{i}\) is the observed value (the yield for that \(p_{T}\) bin), and \(\sigma_{i}\) is the error on \(O_{i}\) (which is assumed to be normally distributed). The second \(\chi^{2}\) equation (Eq. 6.2) is used to test how consistent with zero the yield values are.

Before the \(\gamma/\pi^{0}\)-charged yield values are analyzed, the two \(\chi^{2}\) equations are applied to the charged-particle triggered yield. These results are shown in Tables VI.8 and VI.9. As predicted by the small \(\sim\)3.8% fit error, these data should show a strong agreement with the expected value, and a weak agreement with 0. To get a more detailed mathematical description of how these data tend, 3 \(\chi^{2}\) values are calculated, one up to 4 \(GeV/c((\chi_{f/0}^{2})^{4})\),using the first \(p_{T}\) bin, the next up to 5 \(GeV/c((\chi_{f/0}^{2})^{5})\), using the first and second \(p_{T}\) bins and the last up to 6 \(GeV/c((\chi_{f/0}^{2})^{7})\), using all the \(p_{T}\) bins.

For the \(\gamma\)-triggered correlations, 5 \(\chi^{2}\) values are calculated to show how the the yields trend with increasing trigger \(p_{T}\) (the super-script \(p_{T}\) is the right edge of that \(p_{T}\) bin); \((\chi^{2})^{7}\), \((\chi^{2})^{8}\), \((\chi^{2})^{10}\), \((\chi^{2})^{12}\) and \((\chi^{2})^{16}\). These values can be found in Tables VI.10 and VI.11, and Tables VI.12 & VI.13; they are also plot in Figs. VI.18 and VI.19.

